    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nForward proxy: client-facing, used to bypass restrictions or anonymize. Reverse proxy: server-facing, used for load balancing, caching, security. Both sit between clients and servers.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a forward proxy and a reverse proxy and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Forward proxy: client-facing, used to bypass restrictions or anonymize.\n2. Reverse proxy: server-facing, used for load balancing, caching, security.\n3. Both sit between clients and servers.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Forward proxy: client-facing, used to bypass restrictions or anonymize.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Forward proxy: client-facing, used to bypass restrictions or anonymize.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
