    shortDescription: "Explain RTO and RPO.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRTO (Recovery Time Objective): maximum acceptable downtime after a disaster. RPO (Recovery Point Objective): maximum acceptable data loss (time since last backup). Both guide backup frequency and failover planning.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain RTO and RPO accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. RTO (Recovery Time Objective): maximum acceptable downtime after a disaster.\n2. RPO (Recovery Point Objective): maximum acceptable data loss (time since last backup).\n3. Both guide backup frequency and failover planning.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. RTO (Recovery Time Objective): maximum acceptable downtime after a disaster.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. RTO (Recovery Time Objective): maximum acceptable downtime after a disaster.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
