    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCache-aside (lazy loading), Read-through, Write-through, Write-behind (write-back), and Refresh-ahead. Each has trade-offs in consistency and performance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Cache-aside (lazy loading), Read-through, Write-through, Write-behind (write-back), and Refresh-ahead.\n2. Each has trade-offs in consistency and performance.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Cache-aside (lazy loading), Read-through, Write-through, Write-behind (write-back), and Refresh-ahead.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production platform, what are the different caching strategies affects component boundaries, capacity, latency, consistency, or failure handling. The design should start from traffic and reliability assumptions, then choose the simplest architecture that meets them.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
