    question: "What is the difference between CDN and reverse proxy caching?",
    slug: "cdn-vs-reverse-proxy-cache",
    shortDescription: "Compare caching layers.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCDN caches static assets globally near users. Reverse proxy (like Varnish) caches dynamic responses at the edge of the application, reducing origin server load.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between cdn and reverse proxy caching and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. CDN caches static assets globally near users.\n2. Reverse proxy (like Varnish) caches dynamic responses at the edge of the application, reducing origin server load.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. CDN caches static assets globally near users.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. CDN caches static assets globally near users.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
