    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSQL: relational, schema-defined, ACID, good for complex queries. NoSQL: non-relational, flexible schema, scales horizontally, eventual consistency. Use SQL for structured data, NoSQL for unstructured/high scale.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between sql and nosql databases and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. SQL: relational, schema-defined, ACID, good for complex queries.\n2. NoSQL: non-relational, flexible schema, scales horizontally, eventual consistency.\n3. Use SQL for structured data, NoSQL for unstructured/high scale.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. SQL: relational, schema-defined, ACID, good for complex queries.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. SQL: relational, schema-defined, ACID, good for complex queries.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
