    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIndexes speed up data retrieval at the cost of slower writes and storage. Use B-tree, hash, or bitmap indexes. Choose index based on query patterns.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain database indexing and why is it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Indexes speed up data retrieval at the cost of slower writes and storage.\n2. Use B-tree, hash, or bitmap indexes.\n3. Choose index based on query patterns.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Indexes speed up data retrieval at the cost of slower writes and storage.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Indexes speed up data retrieval at the cost of slower writes and storage.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
