    slug: "join-vs-subquery",
    shortDescription: "Compare query methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJOIN combines rows from multiple tables based on a condition. Subquery is a query nested inside another. JOINS are generally more efficient for large datasets.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between join and subquery and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. JOIN combines rows from multiple tables based on a condition.\n2. Subquery is a query nested inside another.\n3. JOINS are generally more efficient for large datasets.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. JOIN combines rows from multiple tables based on a condition.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. JOIN combines rows from multiple tables based on a condition.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
