    slug: "queue-vs-topic",
    shortDescription: "Compare messaging patterns.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA queue (point-to-point) sends each message to one consumer. A topic (publish-subscribe) broadcasts messages to all subscribers. Queues are for work distribution; topics for event notifications.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a queue and a topic and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A queue (point-to-point) sends each message to one consumer.\n2. A topic (publish-subscribe) broadcasts messages to all subscribers.\n3. Queues are for work distribution; topics for event notifications.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A queue (point-to-point) sends each message to one consumer.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A queue (point-to-point) sends each message to one consumer.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
