    slug: "kafka",
    shortDescription: "Explain Kafka.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nKafka is a distributed streaming platform. It stores streams of records in topics, partitioned across brokers. Producers write to partitions; consumers read from partitions. It offers high throughput, durability, and replayability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Apache Kafka and how does it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Kafka is a distributed streaming platform.\n2. It stores streams of records in topics, partitioned across brokers.\n3. Producers write to partitions; consumers read from partitions.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Kafka is a distributed streaming platform.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Kafka is a distributed streaming platform.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
