    question: "What is TypeScript and what are its main features?",
    slug: "typescript-overview",
    shortDescription: "Define TypeScript and list its key features.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTypeScript is a strongly typed, object-oriented, compiled language that builds on JavaScript. It is a superset of JavaScript, adding static typing, interfaces, generics, enums, and advanced type features. Key features: static type checking, type inference, ES6+ support, tooling (autocompletion, refactoring), and compatibility with plain JavaScript.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain TypeScript and what are its main features accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. TypeScript is a strongly typed, object-oriented, compiled language that builds on JavaScript.\n2. It is a superset of JavaScript, adding static typing, interfaces, generics, enums, and advanced type features.\n3. Key features: static type checking, type inference, ES6+ support, tooling (autocompletion, refactoring), and compatibility with plain JavaScript.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. TypeScript is a strongly typed, object-oriented, compiled language that builds on JavaScript.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
