    question: "What is the difference between TypeScript and JavaScript?",
    slug: "typescript-vs-javascript",
    shortDescription: "Compare TypeScript and JavaScript.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJavaScript is a dynamically typed language; TypeScript adds static typing. TypeScript code is transpiled to JavaScript, so it runs wherever JavaScript runs. TypeScript provides better tooling, catches errors at compile time, and supports modern features with ES target compilation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between typescript and javascript and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. JavaScript is a dynamically typed language; TypeScript adds static typing.\n2. TypeScript code is transpiled to JavaScript, so it runs wherever JavaScript runs.\n3. TypeScript provides better tooling, catches errors at compile time, and supports modern features with ES target compilation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. JavaScript is a dynamically typed language; TypeScript adds static typing.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
