    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTypeScript infers types when not explicitly annotated. It uses the initial value or usage context to determine the type. Example: `let x = 10;` infers `number`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain type inference accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. TypeScript infers types when not explicitly annotated.\n2. It uses the initial value or usage context to determine the type.\n3. Example: `let x = 10;` infers `number`.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. TypeScript infers types when not explicitly annotated.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
