    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`any` disables type checking for a variable, allowing any value. Use it only when migrating from JS or when you truly don't know the type. Prefer `unknown` over `any` for type safety.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `any` type and when should you use it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `any` disables type checking for a variable, allowing any value.\n2. Use it only when migrating from JS or when you truly don't know the type.\n3. Prefer `unknown` over `any` for type safety.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. `any` disables type checking for a variable, allowing any value.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
