    slug: "interface-vs-type",
    shortDescription: "Compare interface and type alias.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nInterfaces can be extended and implemented; type aliases can represent primitives, unions, tuples, etc. Interfaces are generally preferred for object types; type aliases for complex types. Both can be used similarly, but interfaces support declaration merging.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `interface` and `type` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Interfaces can be extended and implemented; type aliases can represent primitives, unions, tuples, etc.\n2. Interfaces are generally preferred for object types; type aliases for complex types.\n3. Both can be used similarly, but interfaces support declaration merging.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Interfaces can be extended and implemented; type aliases can represent primitives, unions, tuples, etc.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
