    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA type predicate is a return type of `value is Type` used in type guard functions. Example: `function isString(value: any): value is string { return typeof value === 'string'; }`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A type predicate is a return type of `value is Type` used in type guard functions.\n2. Example: `function isString(value: any): value is string { return typeof value === 'string'; }`.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. A type predicate is a return type of `value is Type` used in type guard functions.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
