    question: "What is the difference between generic functions and union types?",
    slug: "generic-vs-union",
    shortDescription: "Compare generics and unions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGenerics capture the actual type used, preserving the relationship between arguments and return. Unions allow only common behavior; generics are more precise.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between generic functions and union types and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Generics capture the actual type used, preserving the relationship between arguments and return.\n2. Unions allow only common behavior; generics are more precise.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Generics capture the actual type used, preserving the relationship between arguments and return.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
