    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHigher-order types are types that take other types as parameters, often using generics. They are used to create new types from existing ones (e.g., `Partial<T>`, `Readonly<T>`).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Higher-order types are types that take other types as parameters, often using generics.\n2. They are used to create new types from existing ones (e.g., `Partial<T>`, `Readonly<T>`).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the requested list directly, then go deeper on the item most relevant to the interviewer’s context.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Higher-order types are types that take other types as parameters, often using generics.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
