    slug: "namespace",
    shortDescription: "Explain namespaces.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNamespaces are internal modules used to group related code and avoid naming collisions. They are older and less used now; prefer ES modules.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a namespace in TypeScript accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Namespaces are internal modules used to group related code and avoid naming collisions.\n2. They are older and less used now; prefer ES modules.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Namespaces are internal modules used to group related code and avoid naming collisions.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
