    slug: "namespace-vs-module",
    shortDescription: "Compare namespace and module.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNamespaces are TypeScript-specific and compile to IIFEs; modules are ES standard and are file-based. Modules are recommended for modern code.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a namespace and a module and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Namespaces are TypeScript-specific and compile to IIFEs; modules are ES standard and are file-based.\n2. Modules are recommended for modern code.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a TypeScript application, a developer can reproduce the behavior with a small component or browser interaction and inspect the resulting UI or runtime state. Namespaces are TypeScript-specific and compile to IIFEs; modules are ES standard and are file-based.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Namespaces are TypeScript-specific and compile to IIFEs; modules are ES standard and are file-based.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
