    slug: "external-modules",
    shortDescription: "Explain module resolution.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTypeScript supports `node` and `classic` module resolution. With Node.js, it looks for `node_modules`. Use `module: commonjs` or `esnext` in tsconfig.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you use external modules with typescript, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. TypeScript supports `node` and `classic` module resolution.\n2. With Node.js, it looks for `node_modules`.\n3. Use `module: commonjs` or `esnext` in tsconfig.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. TypeScript supports `node` and `classic` module resolution.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
