    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nBoth are compile-time constructs; runtime performance is identical. However, interfaces are generally faster to check and may be more efficient for large types.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `type` and `interface` in terms of performance and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Both are compile-time constructs; runtime performance is identical.\n2. However, interfaces are generally faster to check and may be more efficient for large types.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Both are compile-time constructs; runtime performance is identical.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
