    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse a generic class: `class Container<T> { private data: T; constructor(data: T) { this.data = data; } get(): T { return this.data; } }`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how would you type a class that can be instantiated with different types, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use a generic class: `class Container<T> { private data: T; constructor(data: T) { this.data = data; } get(): T { return this.data; } }`.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nA function typed as `(userId: string) => User` lets the compiler reject `getUser(42)` before runtime because the argument type is wrong.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Use a generic class: `class Container<T> { private data: T; constructor(data: T) { this.data = data; } get(): T { return this.data; } }`.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
