    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse conditional types: `type Awaited<T> = T extends Promise<infer U>? U: T;`. (Built-in in ES2022: `Awaited<T>`).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how would you type a utility that extracts the value type of a promise, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use conditional types: `type Awaited<T> = T extends Promise<infer U>.\n2. U: T;`.\n3. (Built-in in ES2022: `Awaited<T>`).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nAn `async` function can await a fetch promise; the current function yields while the I/O is pending and continues when the promise settles.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a TypeScript application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Use conditional types: `type Awaited<T> = T extends Promise<infer U>?\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
