    shortDescription: "Compare frontend and backend.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFrontend is everything the user sees and interacts with (HTML, CSS, JS). Backend is server-side logic, databases, and APIs (Node.js, Python, Java).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between frontend and backend and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Frontend is everything the user sees and interacts with (HTML, CSS, JS).\n2. Backend is server-side logic, databases, and APIs (Node.js, Python, Java).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Frontend is everything the user sees and interacts with (HTML, CSS, JS).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Frontend is everything the user sees and interacts with (HTML, CSS, JS).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
