    slug: "script-vs-link",
    shortDescription: "Compare external resources.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`<script>` loads JavaScript (can be external or inline). `<link>` loads external resources like CSS stylesheets, icons, and preconnect.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `<script>` and `<link>` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `<script>` loads JavaScript (can be external or inline).\n2. `<link>` loads external resources like CSS stylesheets, icons, and preconnect.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `<script>` loads JavaScript (can be external or inline).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `<script>` loads JavaScript (can be external or inline).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
