    slug: "class-vs-id",
    shortDescription: "Compare selectors.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nID (`#id`) is unique and can only be used once per page; class (`.class`) can be applied to multiple elements. Classes have lower specificity than IDs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between class and id selectors and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. ID (`#id`) is unique and can only be used once per page; class (`.class`) can be applied to multiple elements.\n2. Classes have lower specificity than IDs.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. ID (`#id`) is unique and can only be used once per page; class (`.class`) can be applied to multiple elements.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. ID (`#id`) is unique and can only be used once per page; class (`.class`) can be applied to multiple elements.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
