    shortDescription: "Explain box model.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe box model consists of: content, padding, border, and margin. Total width = width + padding + border + margin (if `box-sizing: content-box`). Use `box-sizing: border-box` to include padding and border in width.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the CSS box model accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The box model consists of: content, padding, border, and margin.\n2. Total width = width + padding + border + margin (if `box-sizing: content-box`).\n3. Use `box-sizing: border-box` to include padding and border in width.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The box model consists of: content, padding, border, and margin.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The box model consists of: content, padding, border, and margin.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
