    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nClickjacking hides a legitimate button under an invisible overlay. Prevent with `X-Frame-Options: DENY` or `frame-ancestors` CSP directive.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain clickjacking and how to prevent it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Clickjacking hides a legitimate button under an invisible overlay.\n2. Prevent with `X-Frame-Options: DENY` or `frame-ancestors` CSP directive.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Clickjacking hides a legitimate button under an invisible overlay.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Clickjacking hides a legitimate button under an invisible overlay.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
