    slug: "password-storage",
    shortDescription: "Explain password storage.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNever store plaintext passwords. Use strong hashing (bcrypt, Argon2) with salt. Use HTTPS to protect passwords in transit.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you handle password storage securely, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Never store plaintext passwords.\n2. Use strong hashing (bcrypt, Argon2) with salt.\n3. Use HTTPS to protect passwords in transit.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Never store plaintext passwords.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Never store plaintext passwords.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
