    slug: "http1-vs-http2",
    shortDescription: "Compare HTTP versions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTTP/2 introduces multiplexing (multiple requests over one connection), header compression, server push, and binary framing. It improves performance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between http/1.1 and http/2 and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. HTTP/2 introduces multiplexing (multiple requests over one connection), header compression, server push, and binary framing.\n2. It improves performance.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. HTTP/2 introduces multiplexing (multiple requests over one connection), header compression, server push, and binary framing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. HTTP/2 introduces multiplexing (multiple requests over one connection), header compression, server push, and binary framing.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
