    question: "What is the difference between JSP and Servlets?",
    slug: "jsp-vs-servlets",
    shortDescription: "Compare JSP and Servlets.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nServlets are Java classes that handle requests and generate responses programmatically. JSPs are text-based documents that embed Java code in HTML, making them easier for presentation. Servlets are better for business logic and control, JSPs for view rendering. - **Servlets**: Pure Java, better for complex logic, manipulation of request/response, and handling non-HTML content (e.g., JSON, XML). They are compiled classes. - **JSP**: HTML with embedded code, easier for generating dynamic HTML, supports tags (JSTL) to reduce Java code. They are translated into servlets at runtime. Both are used together in MVC: Servlet as controller, JSP as view.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between jsp and servlets and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Servlets are Java classes that handle requests and generate responses programmatically.\n2. JSPs are text-based documents that embed Java code in HTML, making them easier for presentation.\n3. Servlets are better for business logic and control, JSPs for view rendering.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Servlets are Java classes that handle requests and generate responses programmatically.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Servlets are Java classes that handle requests and generate responses programmatically.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
