    question: "What is EL (Expression Language) in JSP?",
    slug: "el-jsp",
    shortDescription: "Explain EL.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEL (Expression Language) is a simple language for accessing data in JSPs, using syntax like `${expression}`. It can access attributes, JavaBeans properties, maps, and call methods. It was introduced to simplify JSPs and replace scriptlets. EL can access objects from page, request, session, and application scopes. It supports operators (arithmetic, relational, logical), and can call methods on objects. It is often used with JSTL. EL also supports implicit objects like `param`, `header`, `cookie`, `initParam`, etc.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain EL (Expression Language) in JSP accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. EL (Expression Language) is a simple language for accessing data in JSPs, using syntax like `${expression}`.\n2. It can access attributes, JavaBeans properties, maps, and call methods.\n3. It was introduced to simplify JSPs and replace scriptlets.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. EL (Expression Language) is a simple language for accessing data in JSPs, using syntax like `${expression}`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. EL (Expression Language) is a simple language for accessing data in JSPs, using syntax like `${expression}`.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
