    question: "What is a `Session` and how is it managed in Java web applications?",
    slug: "session-management",
    shortDescription: "Explain session management.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA `Session` is a user-specific context that persists across multiple requests. In Java web apps, sessions are managed via `HttpSession` object, created by the container. Sessions can be tracked using cookies (JSESSIONID) or URL rewriting. They can store attributes and have a timeout. `HttpSession` is obtained via `request.getSession()`. It allows storing attributes (`setAttribute`, `getAttribute`). Sessions can be invalidated manually or time out (configured in web.xml). Session tracking uses cookies; if cookies are disabled, URL rewriting is used by appending `jsessionid` to URLs. For security, session IDs should be regenerated after login to prevent fixation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a `Session` and how is it managed in Java web applications accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A `Session` is a user-specific context that persists across multiple requests.\n2. In Java web apps, sessions are managed via `HttpSession` object, created by the container.\n3. Sessions can be tracked using cookies (JSESSIONID) or URL rewriting.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A `Session` is a user-specific context that persists across multiple requests.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A `Session` is a user-specific context that persists across multiple requests.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA `Session` is a user-specific context that persists across multiple requests. In Java web apps, sessions are managed via `HttpSession` object, created by the container. Sessions can be tracked using cookies (JSESSIONID) or URL rewriting. They can store attributes and have a timeout. `HttpSession` is obtained via `request.getSession()`. It allows storing attributes (`setAttribute`, `getAttribute`). Sessions can be invalidated manually or time out (configured in web.xml). Session tracking uses cookies; if cookies are disabled, URL rewriting is used by appending `jsessionid` to URLs. For security, session IDs should be regenerated after login to prevent fixation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a `Session` and how is it managed in Java web applications accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A `Session` is a user-specific context that persists across multiple requests.\n2. In Java web apps, sessions are managed via `HttpSession` object, created by the container.\n3. Sessions can be tracked using cookies (JSESSIONID) or URL rewriting.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A `Session` is a user-specific context that persists across multiple requests.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A `Session` is a user-specific context that persists across multiple requests.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
