    question: "What is the difference between `Session` and `Cookie`?",
    slug: "session-vs-cookie",
    shortDescription: "Compare Session and Cookie.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA `Session` stores data on the server side, while a `Cookie` stores data on the client side. Sessions are more secure and can store larger data, but they increase server memory. Cookies are limited in size (4KB) and can be tampered with, but they persist across browser sessions. - **Session**: Server-side storage, accessible via `HttpSession`. Data persists across requests for a user. Can store objects. Secure because data never leaves server. But sessions have memory overhead and need clustering in distributed setups. - **Cookie**: Client-side storage, sent with every HTTP request. Limited size (about 4KB). Can be set with expiration. Easily accessible to client.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `session` and `cookie` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A `Session` stores data on the server side, while a `Cookie` stores data on the client side.\n2. Sessions are more secure and can store larger data, but they increase server memory.\n3. Cookies are limited in size (4KB) and can be tampered with, but they persist across browser sessions.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A `Session` stores data on the server side, while a `Cookie` stores data on the client side.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A `Session` stores data on the server side, while a `Cookie` stores data on the client side.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
