    slug: "servletcontext-vs-servletconfig",
    shortDescription: "Compare ServletContext and ServletConfig.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`ServletConfig` provides configuration for a single servlet (init parameters). `ServletContext` provides access to application-wide data and resources, shared across all servlets. `ServletContext` is accessible via `getServletContext()`, while `ServletConfig` is passed to `init()`. - `ServletConfig`: Per-servlet configuration, defined in `web.xml` with `<init-param>`. Obtained in `init()` method. Used for servlet-specific settings. - `ServletContext`: Application-level, accessible to all servlets. Used for global parameters, shared resources (e.g., database connection pool), and logging.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a `ServletContext` and how is it different from `ServletConfig` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `ServletConfig` provides configuration for a single servlet (init parameters).\n2. `ServletContext` provides access to application-wide data and resources, shared across all servlets.\n3. `ServletContext` is accessible via `getServletContext()`, while `ServletConfig` is passed to `init()`.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `ServletConfig` provides configuration for a single servlet (init parameters).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `ServletConfig` provides configuration for a single servlet (init parameters).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
