    question: "What are `application` scopes in JSP? Explain page, request, session, application.",
    slug: "jsp-scopes",
    shortDescription: "Explain JSP scopes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nScopes define the accessibility of attributes in JSP/Servlets. `page` is limited to the current JSP page, `request` to the current request, `session` to the user session, and `application` to the entire web application. Each scope has a corresponding object: `pageContext`, `request`, `session`, `application`. - **Page**: Only valid within the current JSP page. Use `pageContext.setAttribute()`. Rarely used. - **Request**: Valid for the current HTTP request, can be forwarded to other resources. Use `request.setAttribute()`. - **Session**: Valid for the user's session across multiple requests. Use `session.setAttribute()`. - **Application**: Valid for the entire.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Scopes define the accessibility of attributes in JSP/Servlets.\n2. `page` is limited to the current JSP page, `request` to the current request, `session` to the user session, and `application` to the entire web application.\n3. Each scope has a corresponding object: `pageContext`, `request`, `session`, `application`.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Scopes define the accessibility of attributes in JSP/Servlets.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. Scopes define the accessibility of attributes in JSP/Servlets.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
