    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWebSocket provides full-duplex, long-lived connections for real-time communication. HTTP/2 is a protocol upgrade that improves performance via multiplexing and server push, but it is still request-response based. WebSocket is better for real-time bidirectional messaging, while HTTP/2 is better for regular web traffic with many requests. - **WebSocket**: Persistent connection, low overhead after handshake, supports push from server at any time. Ideal for chat, gaming, financial tickers. - **HTTP/2**: Uses binary framing, multiplexing, server push, but still follows request-response semantics. Better for typical web page loading with many resources.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `websocket` and `http/2` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. WebSocket provides full-duplex, long-lived connections for real-time communication.\n2. HTTP/2 is a protocol upgrade that improves performance via multiplexing and server push, but it is still request-response based.\n3. WebSocket is better for real-time bidirectional messaging, while HTTP/2 is better for regular web traffic with many requests.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. WebSocket provides full-duplex, long-lived connections for real-time communication.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. WebSocket provides full-duplex, long-lived connections for real-time communication.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
