    question: "What is the purpose of `web.xml` in Java web applications?",
    slug: "web-xml",
    shortDescription: "Explain web.xml.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`web.xml` is the deployment descriptor for Java web applications. It defines servlet mappings, filters, listeners, welcome files, session timeout, and security constraints. In Servlet 3.0+, annotations can replace many entries. `web.xml` is located in `WEB-INF/`. It is used to configure: servlets (`<servlet>` and `<servlet-mapping>`), filters, listeners, context parameters, error pages, session config, security roles, etc. With annotations like `@WebServlet`, `@WebFilter`, `@WebListener`, you can reduce XML. However, `web.xml` can still be used for overriding or external configuration.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the purpose of `web.xml` in Java web applications accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `web.xml` is the deployment descriptor for Java web applications.\n2. It defines servlet mappings, filters, listeners, welcome files, session timeout, and security constraints.\n3. In Servlet 3.0+, annotations can replace many entries.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `web.xml` is the deployment descriptor for Java web applications.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `web.xml` is the deployment descriptor for Java web applications.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
