    question: "What is the difference between `forward` and `sendRedirect`?",
    slug: "forward-vs-redirect",
    shortDescription: "Compare forward and sendRedirect.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`forward` happens on the server side, without client involvement, and keeps the same request and URL. `sendRedirect` sends a 302 response to the client, causing a new request to the new URL, and changes the browser URL. - **forward**: Server-side transfer, same request object (attributes preserved), fast, browser URL unchanged. Used for internal navigation (e.g., from servlet to JSP). - **sendRedirect**: Client-side redirection, new request (attributes lost unless stored in session or cookies), slower, browser URL changes. Used to redirect to external URLs or after POST to avoid duplicate submissions (POST-Redirect-GET pattern).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `forward` and `sendredirect` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `forward` happens on the server side, without client involvement, and keeps the same request and URL.\n2. `sendRedirect` sends a 302 response to the client, causing a new request to the new URL, and changes the browser URL.\n3. - **forward**: Server-side transfer, same request object (attributes preserved), fast, browser URL unchanged.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `forward` happens on the server side, without client involvement, and keeps the same request and URL.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `forward` happens on the server side, without client involvement, and keeps the same request and URL.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
