    question: "What is a `DataSource` and how does it differ from `DriverManager`?",
    slug: "datasource-vs-drivermanager",
    shortDescription: "Compare DataSource and DriverManager.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`DriverManager` is a basic JDBC driver management class. `DataSource` is a more advanced way to get connections, providing connection pooling and distributed transactions. `DataSource` is preferred in enterprise applications. - `DriverManager`: Simple, but doesn't support connection pooling. Creating connections is expensive. It loads drivers statically. Not recommended for production. - `DataSource`: Provides connection pooling via implementations (e.g., Apache DBCP, HikariCP, Tomcat JDBC). Supports JTA for distributed transactions. Obtained via JNDI or programmatically. Improves performance and scalability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a `DataSource` and how does it differ from `DriverManager` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `DriverManager` is a basic JDBC driver management class.\n2. `DataSource` is a more advanced way to get connections, providing connection pooling and distributed transactions.\n3. `DataSource` is preferred in enterprise applications.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `DriverManager` is a basic JDBC driver management class.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `DriverManager` is a basic JDBC driver management class.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
