    shortDescription: "Compare getString and getObject.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`getString()` returns the column value as a `String`, while `getObject()` returns it as an `Object`. `getObject()` is more flexible but requires explicit casting. `getString()` may apply database-specific conversions. - `getString()`: Converts the column to a Java String, regardless of database type. Useful for portability. - `getObject()`: Returns the value as a Java Object, which can be a specific type (e.g., Integer, Date, Array). You can then cast. This avoids conversion overhead but requires knowledge of the column type.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `getstring()` and `getobject()` in `resultset` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `getString()` returns the column value as a `String`, while `getObject()` returns it as an `Object`.\n2. `getObject()` is more flexible but requires explicit casting.\n3. `getString()` may apply database-specific conversions.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `getString()` returns the column value as a `String`, while `getObject()` returns it as an `Object`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `getString()` returns the column value as a `String`, while `getObject()` returns it as an `Object`.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
