    question: "What is SQL injection and how can it be prevented in Java?",
    slug: "sql-injection-prevention",
    shortDescription: "Explain SQL injection and prevention.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSQL injection is a security vulnerability where an attacker injects malicious SQL code into input fields. It can be prevented by using `PreparedStatement` (with parameterized queries), escaping input, and using stored procedures. SQL injection occurs when user input is concatenated directly into SQL strings. This can lead to data theft, deletion, or bypassing authentication. Prevention: - Use `PreparedStatement` with placeholders; the driver escapes input automatically. - Validate and sanitize inputs. - Use stored procedures (if they accept parameters). - Apply least privilege to database accounts.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain SQL injection and how can it be prevented in Java accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. SQL injection is a security vulnerability where an attacker injects malicious SQL code into input fields.\n2. It can be prevented by using `PreparedStatement` (with parameterized queries), escaping input, and using stored procedures.\n3. SQL injection occurs when user input is concatenated directly into SQL strings.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. SQL injection is a security vulnerability where an attacker injects malicious SQL code into input fields.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. SQL injection is a security vulnerability where an attacker injects malicious SQL code into input fields.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
