    shortDescription: "Define Spring and list its modules.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSpring is a comprehensive framework for building enterprise Java applications. Its main modules are: Core Container (IoC, DI), AOP, Data Access (JDBC, ORM), Web (Spring MVC, WebFlux), Security, and Boot for microservices. - **Core Container**: `spring-core`, `spring-beans`, `spring-context` – provides IoC and DI. - **AOP**: `spring-aop` – aspect-oriented programming. - **Data Access**: `spring-jdbc`, `spring-orm`, `spring-tx` – transaction management and ORM integration. - **Web**: `spring-web`, `spring-webmvc`, `spring-webflux` – web layer, REST APIs, reactive. - **Security**: Spring Security – authentication and authorization. - **Boot**: Spring Boot –.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the Spring Framework and what are its main modules accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Spring is a comprehensive framework for building enterprise Java applications.\n2. Its main modules are: Core Container (IoC, DI), AOP, Data Access (JDBC, ORM), Web (Spring MVC, WebFlux), Security, and Boot for microservices.\n3. - **Core Container**: `spring-core`, `spring-beans`, `spring-context` – provides IoC and DI.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Spring is a comprehensive framework for building enterprise Java applications.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Spring is a comprehensive framework for building enterprise Java applications.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
