    question: "What is Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI)?",
    slug: "ioc-di",
    shortDescription: "Explain IoC and DI.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIoC (Inversion of Control) is a principle where the control of objects is transferred to a container. DI (Dependency Injection) is a pattern that implements IoC by injecting dependencies into a class from the outside, rather than the class creating them itself. - **IoC**: The framework (Spring container) manages object creation and lifecycle, rather than the application code. This decouples components. - **DI**: Dependencies are provided to a class (e.g., via constructor, setter, or field injection) by the container. This makes classes testable and flexible.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI) accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. IoC (Inversion of Control) is a principle where the control of objects is transferred to a container.\n2. DI (Dependency Injection) is a pattern that implements IoC by injecting dependencies into a class from the outside, rather than the class creating them itself.\n3. - **IoC**: The framework (Spring container) manages object creation and lifecycle, rather than the application code.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nInject a `PaymentRepository` through a service constructor instead of calling `new PaymentRepository()` inside the service; tests can then supply a fake or mock repository.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. IoC (Inversion of Control) is a principle where the control of objects is transferred to a container.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
