    question: "What is the difference between `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, and `@Controller`?",
    slug: "spring-stereotype-annotations",
    shortDescription: "Compare stereotype annotations.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThey are all Spring stereotypes that register a class as a bean. `@Component` is generic; `@Service` is for service layer; `@Repository` for DAO layer (adds translation of persistence exceptions); `@Controller` for web controllers (with `@ResponseBody` for REST). - `@Component`: generic stereotype for any Spring-managed bean. - `@Service`: indicates a service class (business logic). It's a specialization of `@Component`. - `@Repository`: for DAO/repository classes. It enables persistence exception translation (e.g., converting `SQLException` to Spring's `DataAccessException`). - `@Controller`: for web controllers (MVC). Often used with `@RequestMapping`. -.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@component`, `@service`, `@repository`, and `@controller` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. They are all Spring stereotypes that register a class as a bean.\n2. `@Component` is generic; `@Service` is for service layer; `@Repository` for DAO layer (adds translation of persistence exceptions); `@Controller` for web controllers (with `@ResponseBody` for REST).\n3. - `@Component`: generic stereotype for any Spring-managed bean.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. They are all Spring stereotypes that register a class as a bean.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. They are all Spring stereotypes that register a class as a bean.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
