    question: "What is the difference between constructor injection and setter injection?",
    slug: "constructor-vs-setter-injection",
    shortDescription: "Compare injection types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nConstructor injection uses the class constructor to inject dependencies, ensuring immutability and required dependencies. Setter injection uses setter methods, allowing optional dependencies and reconfiguration. Constructor injection is preferred for mandatory dependencies. - **Constructor**: Dependencies are provided via constructor parameters. Makes them immutable and thread-safe. Forces dependencies to be present. Works well with final fields. Encourages testability. - **Setter**: Uses setter methods. Allows optional dependencies (by not calling setter). Can cause circular dependencies (solved with setter). More verbose. Spring recommends constructor.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between constructor injection and setter injection and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Constructor injection uses the class constructor to inject dependencies, ensuring immutability and required dependencies.\n2. Setter injection uses setter methods, allowing optional dependencies and reconfiguration.\n3. Constructor injection is preferred for mandatory dependencies.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Constructor injection uses the class constructor to inject dependencies, ensuring immutability and required dependencies.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Constructor injection uses the class constructor to inject dependencies, ensuring immutability and required dependencies.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
