    question: "What is `@Autowired` and how does it work?",
    slug: "autowired",
    shortDescription: "Explain @Autowired.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@Autowired` is a Spring annotation that marks a dependency for automatic injection. Spring looks for a bean of the required type and wires it. It can be applied to constructors, setters, fields, or methods. - When Spring sees `@Autowired`, it injects a bean that matches the type (by default). If there are multiple candidates, `@Qualifier` can be used to specify a bean name. - It can be applied to fields (field injection), setters (setter injection), constructors (constructor injection), or any method. - Since Spring 4.3, if a class has a single constructor, `@Autowired` is optional.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `@Autowired` and how does it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@Autowired` is a Spring annotation that marks a dependency for automatic injection.\n2. Spring looks for a bean of the required type and wires it.\n3. It can be applied to constructors, setters, fields, or methods.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `@Autowired` is a Spring annotation that marks a dependency for automatic injection.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@Autowired` is a Spring annotation that marks a dependency for automatic injection.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
