    question: "What is the difference between `@Configuration` and `@Component`?",
    slug: "configuration-vs-component",
    shortDescription: "Compare @Configuration and @Component.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@Configuration` indicates a class that declares beans using `@Bean` methods. It is a Spring-managed bean but with special behavior: it is proxied to ensure `@Bean` methods return singleton beans. `@Component` is a generic stereotype for any bean. - `@Configuration`: Used with `@Bean` to define beans programmatically. The class is a bean itself. The container ensures that `@Bean` methods are enhanced to honor singleton scope (CGLIB proxying). - `@Component`: Marks a class as a bean, discovered via component scanning. It doesn't have the proxy enhancement for `@Bean` methods (though you can still use `@Bean` inside a `@Component` but it won't be proxied).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@configuration` and `@component` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@Configuration` indicates a class that declares beans using `@Bean` methods.\n2. It is a Spring-managed bean but with special behavior: it is proxied to ensure `@Bean` methods return singleton beans.\n3. `@Component` is a generic stereotype for any bean.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@Configuration` indicates a class that declares beans using `@Bean` methods.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@Configuration` indicates a class that declares beans using `@Bean` methods.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
