    shortDescription: "Explain @Transactional.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@Transactional` is a Spring annotation that declares transactional boundaries for a method or class. It uses AOP to start a transaction before the method and commit/rollback after. It supports propagation, isolation, timeout, and read-only settings. - `@Transactional` can be applied to methods or classes. - Spring creates a proxy that handles transaction management. - Attributes: `propagation` (REQUIRED, REQUIRES_NEW, etc.), `isolation` (READ_COMMITTED, etc.), `timeout`, `readOnly`, `rollbackFor`. - By default, it rolls back on `RuntimeException` and `Error`, not on checked exceptions. - It requires a `PlatformTransactionManager` (e.g.,.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `@Transactional` and how does it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@Transactional` is a Spring annotation that declares transactional boundaries for a method or class.\n2. It uses AOP to start a transaction before the method and commit/rollback after.\n3. It supports propagation, isolation, timeout, and read-only settings.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `@Transactional` is a Spring annotation that declares transactional boundaries for a method or class.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@Transactional` is a Spring annotation that declares transactional boundaries for a method or class.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
