    shortDescription: "Define Spring Boot.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSpring Boot is a project built on Spring that simplifies application setup and development. It provides auto-configuration, starter dependencies, embedded servers, and production-ready features (actuator, metrics). It reduces boilerplate and configuration. - **Auto-configuration**: Automatically configures Spring beans based on classpath dependencies. - **Starters**: Dependency descriptors that simplify inclusion of common libraries (e.g., `spring-boot-starter-web`). - **Embedded servers**: Tomcat, Jetty, or Undertow are included, so you can run as a JAR. - **Production features**: Actuator provides endpoints for health, metrics, and monitoring. - **DevTools**:.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks engineering judgment around what is the spring boot and its advantages. A useful answer makes the benefit, cost, and condition for choosing the approach explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Spring Boot is a project built on Spring that simplifies application setup and development.\n2. It provides auto-configuration, starter dependencies, embedded servers, and production-ready features (actuator, metrics).\n3. It reduces boilerplate and configuration.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nState the benefit, the cost, and the constraint that decides whether the trade-off is worth accepting.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Spring Boot is a project built on Spring that simplifies application setup and development.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Spring Boot is a project built on Spring that simplifies application setup and development.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Listing benefits and drawbacks without saying when they matter.\n- Treating one approach as universally superior.\n- Ignoring operational cost, maintenance, or failure behavior.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nName the constraint that makes the trade-off acceptable, then state when you would deliberately choose another approach.",
