    slug: "spring-data-jpa",
    shortDescription: "Explain Spring Data JPA.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSpring Data JPA is a Spring module that provides repository support for JPA. It reduces boilerplate code by generating implementations for repository interfaces. It differs from JPA by adding a higher-level abstraction, including query methods, pagination, and auditing. - **JPA**: Specification for ORM in Java; requires implementation (Hibernate, EclipseLink). - **Spring Data JPA**: Provides `Repository` interface hierarchy (`JpaRepository`, `CrudRepository`, `PagingAndSortingRepository`). It automatically implements CRUD methods based on naming conventions (`findBy.`), supports `@Query` for custom JPQL, and provides pagination and sorting.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Spring Data JPA and how does it differ from JPA accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Spring Data JPA is a Spring module that provides repository support for JPA.\n2. It reduces boilerplate code by generating implementations for repository interfaces.\n3. It differs from JPA by adding a higher-level abstraction, including query methods, pagination, and auditing.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Spring Data JPA is a Spring module that provides repository support for JPA.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Spring Data JPA is a Spring module that provides repository support for JPA.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
