    question: "What is the difference between `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToMany`?",
    slug: "manytoone-vs-onetomany",
    shortDescription: "Compare @ManyToOne and @OneToMany.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@ManyToOne` defines a many-to-one relationship from the child entity to the parent entity. `@OneToMany` defines a one-to-many relationship from the parent to the children. They are often used together as a bidirectional relationship. - `@ManyToOne`: Usually on the child side, with `@JoinColumn` to specify foreign key. - `@OneToMany`: Usually on the parent side, with `mappedBy` attribute to point to the field on the child side that maps the relationship. - The side with `@ManyToOne` is typically the owning side; the `@OneToMany` side uses `mappedBy` to indicate it's the inverse side.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@manytoone` and `@onetomany` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@ManyToOne` defines a many-to-one relationship from the child entity to the parent entity.\n2. `@OneToMany` defines a one-to-many relationship from the parent to the children.\n3. They are often used together as a bidirectional relationship.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@ManyToOne` defines a many-to-one relationship from the child entity to the parent entity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@ManyToOne` defines a many-to-one relationship from the child entity to the parent entity.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
