    slug: "jwt-spring-security",
    shortDescription: "Explain JWT in Spring Security.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJWT (JSON Web Token) is a compact, URL-safe token format for securely transmitting claims between parties. In Spring Security, JWT is used for stateless authentication: the client sends the token in the `Authorization` header, and Spring Security validates it via a filter. - JWT consists of header, payload (claims), and signature. It is self-contained and can carry user info. - In Spring Security, you can implement a `OncePerRequestFilter` that extracts the JWT, validates it, and sets the `Authentication` in the `SecurityContext`. - You can use libraries like `jjwt` or `Nimbus JOSE`. - Typically used for REST APIs where session is not needed.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain JWT and how is it used with Spring Security accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. JWT (JSON Web Token) is a compact, URL-safe token format for securely transmitting claims between parties.\n2. In Spring Security, JWT is used for stateless authentication: the client sends the token in the `Authorization` header, and Spring Security validates it via a filter.\n3. - JWT consists of header, payload (claims), and signature.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, an engineer can apply the control to an actual request or resource boundary and verify that an unauthorized action is rejected. JWT (JSON Web Token) is a compact, URL-safe token format for securely transmitting claims between parties.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. JWT (JSON Web Token) is a compact, URL-safe token format for securely transmitting claims between parties.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
