    question: "What is the difference between `@ConfigurationProperties` and `@Value`?",
    slug: "configprops-vs-value",
    shortDescription: "Compare @ConfigurationProperties and @Value.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@ConfigurationProperties` binds external properties to a Java bean in a type-safe way, supporting complex structures. `@Value` injects individual values. `@ConfigurationProperties` is preferred for grouping related settings. - `@ConfigurationProperties`: Maps properties with a common prefix to fields of a POJO. Supports validation, nested properties, and lists. Provides type safety and structured configuration. - `@Value`: Injects single values, supports SpEL, good for simple or one-off values.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@configurationproperties` and `@value` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@ConfigurationProperties` binds external properties to a Java bean in a type-safe way, supporting complex structures.\n2. `@Value` injects individual values.\n3. `@ConfigurationProperties` is preferred for grouping related settings.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@ConfigurationProperties` binds external properties to a Java bean in a type-safe way, supporting complex structures.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@ConfigurationProperties` binds external properties to a Java bean in a type-safe way, supporting complex structures.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
